#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125429} :activity/id #uuid "f4d9b1d3-776c-5de2-b62c-cbb016b96f1e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048726 :topic/id #uuid "b8f25367-71a5-50d2-9e6e-d239937a1fac" :topic/math-academy-id 1972} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125429 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125794 :tutorial/content "The characteristic equation of a square matrix $A$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0.\n$$\n\nOn the left-hand side of this equation, we have the expression \n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I),\n$$\n\n which we call the **characteristic polynomial** of the matrix $A.$\n\nFor instance, let's find the characteristic polynomial of the matrix ![](images/1d/1d6d0bdedbb25bbc41759e443cdc0f6a207ef5cbfa4761b05e1a1a2eda24cbec.svg)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =det(C-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}-8-\\lambda  & -9 \\\\ 6 & 7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =(-8-\\lambda )\\cdot (7-\\lambda )-(-9)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "25396b96-19ec-592d-b1c4-3c6fa172ef08" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3580 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9083e01a-4e57-589b-9b08-48ecc4af0075" :step/math-academy-id 20133 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125433}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125430 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125795 :tutorial/content "The **Cayley-Hamilton theorem** states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $\\,n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix whose characteristic polynomial is* \n\n$$\np(\\lambda) = \\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I) = (-1)^n\\lambda^n + c_{n-1}\\lambda^{n-1}+\\cdots+c_1\\lambda + c_0.\n$$\n\n*Then, the matrix $A$ is a solution of the characteristic equation $p(\\lambda) = 0$.* \n\nThat is to say, if we substitute $A$ into the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda)$, taking $A^0 = I,$ then we have\n\n$$\np(A) = (-1)^nA^n + c_{n-1}A^{n-1}+\\cdots+c_1A + c_0I = O,\n$$\n\nwhere $O$ is the zero-matrix. \n\nFor example, according to this theorem, for the matrix \n![](images/d2/d24f480293267a9ddc28b03626e7bde6f01bd62a136fed17f8580bf1ea34d776.svg)\nwhose characteristic polynomial is \n\n$$\np(\\lambda)=\\lambda^2 +\\lambda -2,\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\np(A) = A^2 +A -2I =O.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "86101838-75af-5a3f-9dae-1e63a94c77a1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3581 :tutorial/title "The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "7b3b4f95-8159-5533-bd80-db194716f75b" :step/math-academy-id 20134 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125432}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125431 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124536 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124345 :question/id #uuid "c262f28d-bfd0-5c78-bce7-d2ae314052e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5510"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "152243fa-e0a5-551d-92d9-b470789087c7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430412}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Inverse of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Its Powers"} :step/id #uuid "d9e0bce9-63bf-58e4-af9b-2bb5b5d8e03c" :step/math-academy-id 20135 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125502}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125432 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124537 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124346 :question/id #uuid "8e6d3017-d05d-5a2d-8043-c827e612476e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5511"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2867571-c1ae-5ea0-b784-22f444c8db38" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351503} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430413}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Power of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Smaller Powers"} :step/id #uuid "1c6e6482-d051-50c1-b91d-40d9a95a380d" :step/math-academy-id 20136 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125431}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125433 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125796 :tutorial/content "What conclusions can we make regarding the characteristic polynomial of the general $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/8e/8e26937ef1981804a548e92a4041bd8672cc75c7c5cc6efa0c0c2694c0736791.svg)\n\nFirst, let's find the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\left|\\begin{aligned}a-\\lambda  & b \\\\ c & d-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\right| & =0 \\\\ (a-\\lambda )(d-\\lambda )-bc & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-(a+d)\\lambda +(ad-bc) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the constant term is the determinant of the matrix: \n\n$$\n\\det(A) = ad-bc\n$$\n\n \n\nThe coefficient of $\\lambda$ is the sum of the diagonal entries of $A$ taken with a negative sign. The sum of the diagonal entries of a matrix $A$ is called the **trace** of $A,$ and we denote it as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{tr}(A) = a+d.\n$$\n\nSo, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$, the characteristic polynomial is\n\n$$\np(\\lambda) = \\lambda^2 -\\mathrm{tr}(A)\\lambda + \\det(A).\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result can be extended to the general case of $n \\times n$ matrices. If $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix, then its characteristic polynomial takes the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =det(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =(-1)^{n}{\\lambda }^{n}+{c}_{n-1}{\\lambda }^{n-1}+⋯+{c}_{1}\\lambda +{c}_{0} \\\\  & =(-1)^{n}{\\lambda }^{n}+{{(-1)^{n-1}\\text{tr}(A)}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{n-1}}{\\lambda }^{n-1}+⋯+{c}_{1}\\lambda +{{det(A)}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{0}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{n-1}=(-1)^{n-1}\\text{tr}(A)\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{0}=det(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1c54b7a6-10ea-5c57-ad4e-245a308d4790" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3582 :tutorial/title "The Characteristic Polynomial for 2x2 Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "4e500723-739c-51c7-b82e-8727a24a1b1f" :step/math-academy-id 20137 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125455}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125434 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124538 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124347 :question/id #uuid "ce724b3b-f440-5c1f-86a6-c50a54931233" :question/math-academy-id "e-5512"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050117}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392225}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "0fed3d43-139d-505f-8482-70f3be0516d6" :step/math-academy-id 20138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125455 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124552 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124361 :question/id #uuid "95b44b17-b796-51eb-93dd-4ee4b2e5c3f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5894"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87febdb4-5f54-5aff-b84a-1b05359d6b57" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050669}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486439}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum and Product of Eigenvalues of a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "f3faf291-dd20-56c9-bc3d-8482113b6764" :step/math-academy-id 21546 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125430}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125502 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125824 :tutorial/content "If we want to evaluate a high-degree polynomial for a given matrix input, it can take a lot of time. For example, suppose we wanted to evaluate the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^{10} - 5x^9 - 2x^8 + x + 2\n$$\n\nfor the input ![](images/0f/0f9bff826261a956305962b4ca945ed91012cc5042e6ffe92a4b5f1ce13d3237.svg) Computing the $10$th power of this matrix would be a lot of work!\n\nLuckily, there is a way to speed up the process. The trick is to use Cayley-Hamilton theorem, which tells us that every matrix is a solution to its characteristic equation.\n\nFor the given matrix ![](images/65/6514096b22aed47005e5ff59c47e3cec701aed5b120b0022b186f1bec941d953.svg) the characteristic polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-6 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the Cayley-Hamilton theorem tells us that\n\n$$\np(A) = A^2 - 5A - 2I = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, observe that the polynomial $f(x)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{10}-5{x}^{9}-2{x}^{8}+x+2 \\\\  & ={x}^{8}({x}^{2}-5x-2)+x+2 \\\\  & ={x}^{8}p(x)+x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen we have\n\n$$\nf(A) = A^8 p(A) + A + 2I,\n$$\n\nand because $p(A) = 0,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & ={A}^{8}\\cdot 0+A+2I \\\\  & =0+A+2I \\\\  & =A+2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the computation is much faster:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =A+2I \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** Other times, it may not be so obvious how to group the terms of $f(x)$ to reveal $p(x).$ In the general case, it's best to divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division, and use the result\n\n$$\nf(x) = q(x) p(x) + r(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)$ is the quotient and $r(x)$ is the remainder. Let's see an example of this." :tutorial/id #uuid "769ceb10-1667-52b6-8a89-b4c7ca835276" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4448 :tutorial/title "Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem to Quickly Evaluate a Matrix Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "3167e19f-42e7-5fc7-8ee9-fb73a5fa3da3" :step/math-academy-id 27370 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125434}}] :activity/title "The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124757}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440844} :activity/id #uuid "08986419-9a71-537f-9305-f8e778b26413" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048726 :topic/id #uuid "b8f25367-71a5-50d2-9e6e-d239937a1fac" :topic/math-academy-id 1972} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440842 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354446 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b7c0e75-3d49-5406-9041-ede1d5bfdf47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351962} {:answer/id #uuid "33bb1eb5-07b0-5c72-9e4a-a39b341c088c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352048} {:answer/id #uuid "6b960fbb-7fd4-564b-88d9-596a86197fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352519} {:answer/id #uuid "a471234f-1f9f-56ed-9886-d314b4205faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352971} {:answer/id #uuid "e3378a1b-e1c9-5e1d-b90b-a6157d273dbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353544}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351962} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba728d29-f974-5f4e-bf33-9c1c26f466d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "83ac805a-2248-5c38-94ec-8370eb490b39" :question/math-academy-id "q-85928" :question/problem "Given that $\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{A}^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-7A+8I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-7A+8I) & =O \\\\ A-7I+8{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{7}{8}I-\\frac{1}{8}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{7}{8},$ $q=-\\frac{1}{8}$ and\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{7}{8}+(-\\frac{1}{8})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "37974a8d-1c28-54c7-b296-b5b3c2e14d09" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440845}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440843 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354443 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23996cc9-567a-51b9-98b0-2f94757720ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{A}^{2}-3A+16I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351889} {:answer/id #uuid "3b9440a6-7475-5119-a281-5c4281a46f22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{A}^{2}-3A+6I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352117} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e14de5-77a9-57b7-8420-3ce37cbf93ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3A-16I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352952} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a9477d-b279-5e07-bb55-bcddccdb5c4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{A}^{2}+3A-16I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353124} {:answer/id #uuid "dedacf6f-daed-557d-84e3-a6bbb9be9999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3A+16I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353503}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351889} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb1e7ca4-8013-510f-a476-bdfe0c462d57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "03d6fc6a-0f72-5d35-ba67-c4e4d25be4dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-85924" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 3$ matrix with the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{3}-{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -16.$ Then, ${A}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ {A}^{3}-{A}^{2}+3A-16I & =O \\\\ {A}^{3} & ={A}^{2}-3A+16I.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "864708dc-01cf-5025-81a5-3d649155dedc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440842}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440844 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28cc5ee2-6da9-52c1-a92b-8edb26634139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324300} {:answer/id #uuid "47e93b3f-33d7-5090-b1fc-02844e927bff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324553} {:answer/id #uuid "a44579f4-a9e4-53da-a506-9eefbb4bf2e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325367} {:answer/id #uuid "bd8f6f17-d42e-553d-a74b-0e76268b5611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325601} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b95922-aad1-5b77-a0d0-e6a698ab713b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325792}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324300} :answer-field/id #uuid "36035083-9336-58a8-9968-10256743cb12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "688a2834-9fcd-5275-97a0-97e7aaa1b79c" :question/math-academy-id "q-38704" :question/problem "Given that $\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{A}^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-9 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-8A+6I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-8A+6I) & =O \\\\ A-8I+6{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ 6{A}^{-1} & =8I-A \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{4}{3}I-\\frac{1}{6}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{4}{3},$ $q=-\\frac{1}{6}$ and\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{4}{3}+(-\\frac{1}{6})=\\frac{7}{6}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8b979659-38ec-5258-8754-0f5aa34455d5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440843}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440845 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c632238-e62f-5f74-b0da-ba8a2b7c7882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}21 & 0 \\\\ 72 & -27\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351821} {:answer/id #uuid "470c7209-55c8-5c45-abce-7fe3f84f4c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}21 & 27 \\\\ 0 & -72\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352224} {:answer/id #uuid "5e13d16b-8e89-589c-b83e-8c447ea31fb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}27 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352401} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3d6b3f-3399-57a6-94b9-b1ab92b58f8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}21 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -27\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353035} {:answer/id #uuid "dd1b1292-04d5-5403-8881-a67fccaa7676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -12\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353490}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353035} :answer-field/id #uuid "af398d18-f5a4-5a12-bc2f-96080c170593" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e1de5236-83e9-5753-abb0-71abaf264e50" :question/math-academy-id "q-85993" :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $f(x)={x}^{21}-16{x}^{19}+6x-3$, find $f(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4-\\lambda  & 12 \\\\ 0 & -4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda ) \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"left\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,{x}^{19}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{2}-16\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"left\" colspan=\"7\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{21}\\,-\\,16{x}^{19}\\,+\\,6x\\,\\,-\\,\\,3\\phantom{|}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{21}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$16{x}^{19}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$6x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(6x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{19}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(6A-3I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(6A-3I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(6A-3I) \\\\  & =6A-3I \\\\  & =6\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}24 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -24\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}21 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -27\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9ee92d90-8ec9-5c83-abd0-9bf2b158360d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440846}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440846 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272142 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "025a5c1a-4f6e-5e42-8dce-96a2fd08178a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}25 & -42 \\\\ 14 & -31\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269289} {:answer/id #uuid "23310913-8cd2-557c-be76-7e2691fe0ee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}31 & 14 \\\\ 42 & -25\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269575} {:answer/id #uuid "9142a6e7-f5cb-5d0d-bcaf-2c7c6f384044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 14 \\\\ -42 & 30\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270533} {:answer/id #uuid "efb97e7a-5224-5bc1-9035-2d0d54445cd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}25 & 12 \\\\ -24 & -31\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271376} {:answer/id #uuid "f7416ab1-633c-5a86-bade-cffdf59d2d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}25 & 14 \\\\ -42 & -31\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271438}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271438} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d72f403-dcf6-5b06-9505-8ae246f18a3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "af2db92b-ede0-52d7-a7d4-1043a63d9db0" :question/math-academy-id "q-140602" :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $\\,f(x)={x}^{16}-4{x}^{14}+7x-3\\,$, find $f(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )+12 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"left\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,{x}^{14}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{2}-4\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"left\" colspan=\"7\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{16}\\,-\\,4{x}^{14}\\,+\\,7x\\,-\\,3\\phantom{|}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{16}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$4{x}^{14}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$7x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(7x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{14}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(7A-3I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(7A-3I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(7A-3I) \\\\  & =7A-3I \\\\  & =7\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}28 & 14 \\\\ -42 & -28\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}25 & 14 \\\\ -42 & -31\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a1707eef-06e0-5503-8342-6b9421462ee5"}] :activity/title "The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440841}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486487} :activity/id #uuid "335bb2d0-e529-5cbe-ad74-695b640df1d6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048726 :topic/id #uuid "b8f25367-71a5-50d2-9e6e-d239937a1fac" :topic/math-academy-id 1972} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486472 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17bf09fd-3fd5-5fa9-bd7c-9bb8300133ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}I-\\frac{1}{2}A-\\frac{3}{2}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324178} {:answer/id #uuid "2196ad3b-dc18-575d-9ac5-f3e82f2cf1f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}I-\\frac{1}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324248} {:answer/id #uuid "ae47339b-004d-5c20-8d9d-8aab1f4f98f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2I-A-2{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325449} {:answer/id #uuid "bc9f773a-008b-571e-8538-a3402ba6e607" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}I-\\frac{7}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325590} {:answer/id #uuid "d899671a-8147-5a25-9c47-966430b53806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9I-A-{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325855}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324248} :answer-field/id #uuid "f00c9e90-925d-5d82-b4f9-f2d77c2b5637" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0d383f46-698f-5924-8d8d-4466dfa087b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-40168" :question/problem "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${A}^{-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-4-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((-4-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )+3) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3}+{A}^{2}-7A+2I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{3}+{A}^{2}-7A+2I) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}+A-7I+2{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ 2{A}^{-1} & =7I-A-{A}^{2} \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{7}{2}I-\\frac{1}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "29abcc68-95f8-5957-a518-0a35c26f28c7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486519}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486479 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "486030d8-debf-5bf3-8646-937755b93331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2A+{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324558} {:answer/id #uuid "7cb39a68-d9fa-5afc-a46e-1646bc050802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{A}^{2}-A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325023} {:answer/id #uuid "8f700b32-f8f5-50c2-8448-a2e3950f6cf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{A}^{2}-I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325188} {:answer/id #uuid "d2abfec0-1ad1-5263-bbad-6dc6f697cbf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2A-I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325799} {:answer/id #uuid "ec99ac0a-a7c2-59e7-88f3-37b0874d74a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2A-I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326052}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326052} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ae27e9b-e1c9-591e-ae97-64fbf12bf64f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "241347e1-d70b-502b-97bb-5ffa2634f0a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-38723" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 3$ matrix with the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=-{\\lambda }^{3}-2\\lambda -1.$ Then, ${A}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ -{A}^{3}-2A-I & =O \\\\ {A}^{3} & =-2A-I.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4d30d155-c0af-5612-bddd-5c0adca752a1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486502}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486487 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323940 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d94a386-0e3f-5fd6-82d2-7e3b9d7cbeec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321252} {:answer/id #uuid "2cbc3e40-f109-58b8-872a-95626283c9f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321493} {:answer/id #uuid "389d0b0e-b88a-5ab8-9fa3-85ae37139eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321578} {:answer/id #uuid "73fc0bbe-4d33-5b94-bad1-7a748598633e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322053} {:answer/id #uuid "f9936d7e-e12d-5d02-a132-90f3f19d81e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323111}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321252} :answer-field/id #uuid "399304a9-7f1c-5273-8e27-ae2b68d805f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a72555f5-f964-563d-a2c6-2e5bad5e5508" :question/math-academy-id "q-37778" :question/problem "Given that $\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{A}^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-3 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-4A+I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-4A+I) & =O \\\\ A-4I+{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =4I-A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=4,$ $q=-1$ and\n\n$$\np+q=4+(-1)=3.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "712962aa-1f8a-5106-8afe-8f1d72596893" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486479}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486502 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee48dc36-244e-5623-8162-9e5def5de475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}18 & -22 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392227} {:answer/id #uuid "4e10d15f-edfd-50e4-802f-39336ec76684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & -22 \\\\ 6 & 12\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392228} {:answer/id #uuid "7b98b20b-4948-5630-912d-d10854621cc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -22 \\\\ 6 & 18\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392229} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ce0307-f7b7-5df0-93a8-a3b0f9edcea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -22 \\\\ 18 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392230} {:answer/id #uuid "52829169-8b9a-5477-a7ca-27875b4c0680" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-22 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 18\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392231}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392229} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2234961-7a5f-5c89-bdd3-f09c2cf9ab03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4c6e1cb3-d3e5-5ac7-9b61-9f6c0c45ae57" :question/math-academy-id "q-38728" :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 11 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $f(x)={x}^{15}-3{x}^{13}+{x}^{3}-5x+6$, find $f(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}6-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ -3 & -6-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(6-\\lambda )(-6-\\lambda )+33 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"left\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,{x}^{13}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$+$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{2}-3\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"left\" colspan=\"7\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{15}-3{x}^{13}+{x}^{3}-5x+6\\phantom{|}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{15}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,\\,\\,-$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,\\,\\,3{x}^{13}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,5x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,+$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,3x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,2x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,+$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(-2x+6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{13}+x.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(-2A+6I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(-2A+6I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(-2A+6I) \\\\  & =-2A+6I \\\\  & =-2\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 11 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}+6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & -22 \\\\ 6 & 12\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -22 \\\\ 6 & 18\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "aa57e7bc-ae6a-50ca-b096-5a8dfd3e3450" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486472}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486519 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "069c8840-8426-5a49-8cd5-194f9542f047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485779} {:answer/id #uuid "2e481c8d-1f08-54f2-a266-5bbd2762473f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485851} {:answer/id #uuid "93d03cdd-069e-5ebe-b2a7-900eec2dd6d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486030} {:answer/id #uuid "a74c6630-89ff-50d5-a8b8-e49610f1abd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486067} {:answer/id #uuid "b94c247d-0ad1-594f-abae-d08d3203dd0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "47" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486099}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486099} :answer-field/id #uuid "f58af114-b2ae-5846-9a75-5301fb89508f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c746a2db-cfe0-519b-a0f3-936f85b88bb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-140520" :question/problem "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{bmatrix},$ find the product of the eigenvalues of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's Formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the product of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the determinant of $A.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} & =det(A) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5\\cdot 7-(-4)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =35+12 \\\\  & =47.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f73f0739-b0b6-5a0b-9fac-ab5fdff3e3e1"}] :activity/title "The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485755}]}
